'''Egyptian Arabic''', locally known as '''Colloquial Egyptian''',{{efn|{{langx|arz|العامية المصرية}}, {{IPA|arz|el.ʕæmˈmejjæ l.mɑsˤˈɾejjɑ|}}<ref>{{cite book|last=سبيرو|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fa0azQEACAAJ&q=%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9+%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9|script-title=ar:قاموس اللهجة العامية المصرية، عربي – إنكليزي|date=1999|publisher=مكتبة لبنان ناشرون|language=ar|access-date=2020-10-25|archive-date=2023-03-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326133756/https://books.google.com/books?id=fa0azQEACAAJ&q=%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9+%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.net/news/cultureandart/2019/2/12/%D8%AA%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%83%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%B7%D9%8A%D9%81-%D8%B7%D9%87-%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%8A%D9%86-%D9%88%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%82%D8%A8%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%82%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1|script-title=ar:تتويج رواية مكتوبة بالعامية.. طيف طه حسين ومستقبل الثقافة بمصر|website=www.aljazeera.net|language=ar|access-date=2020-02-26|archive-date=2019-07-04|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190704055503/https://www.aljazeera.net/news/cultureandart/2019/2/12/%D8%AA%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%83%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%B7%D9%8A%D9%81-%D8%B7%D9%87-%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%8A%D9%86-%D9%88%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%82%D8%A8%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%82%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://akhbarelyom.com/news/newdetails/590075/1/%D8%B7%D9%87-%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%8A%D9%86..%D8%B9%D8%B4%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D8%AD%D9%89-%D9%88%D9%83%D8%B1%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%B9%D9%89-%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%81%D9%87%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%86%D8%AC%D9%8A%D9%84-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B1%D8%A2%D9%86|script-title=ar:طه حسين..عشق الفصحى وكره العامية ودعى لتفهم التوارة والإنجيل والقرآن|last=Musa|first=Heba|work=بوابة اخبار اليوم |date=15 November 2016|script-website=ar:بوابة اخبار اليوم|access-date=2020-02-26|archive-date=2022-04-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220408104905/https://akhbarelyom.com/news/newdetails/590075/1/%D8%B7%D9%87-%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%8A%D9%86..%D8%B9%D8%B4%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D8%AD%D9%89-%D9%88%D9%83%D8%B1%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%B9%D9%89-%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%81%D9%87%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%86%D8%AC%D9%8A%D9%84-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B1%D8%A2%D9%86|url-status=live}}</ref>}} or simply as '''Masri''',{{efn|{{langx|arz|مصرى<!--Egyptian variant of final ى-->|link=no}}, {{literal translation|'''Egyptian'''}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://etoninstitute.com/blog/language/different-arabic-dialects|title=Different Arabic Dialects Spoken Around the Arab World|date=April 15, 2018|access-date=July 23, 2019|archive-date=November 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201127132549/https://etoninstitute.com/blog/language/different-arabic-dialects|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://enterprise.press/stories/2017/08/29/disney-returns-to-using-egyptian-dialect-in-dubbing-movies/|title=Disney returns to using Egyptian dialect in dubbing movies|website=Enterprise|access-date=2019-05-31|archive-date=2020-11-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201119135519/https://enterprise.press/stories/2017/08/29/disney-returns-to-using-egyptian-dialect-in-dubbing-movies/|url-status=live}}</ref>}} is the most widely spoken [[vernacular]] [[Varieties of Arabic|Arabic variety]] in [[Egypt]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/languages-spoken-in-egypt.html|title=Languages Spoken In Egypt|website=WorldAtlas|date=25 April 2017|language=en|access-date=2019-09-13|archive-date=2020-11-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201119135525/https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/languages-spoken-in-egypt.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jjiZAAAACAAJ&q=egyptian+colloquial+arabic|title=Egyptian Colloquial Arabic|last=Ondras|first=Frantisek|date=2005-04-26|publisher=Czech Institute of Egyptology|isbn=978-80-86277-36-3|language=en|access-date=2020-10-25|archive-date=2023-03-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326133733/https://books.google.com/books?id=jjiZAAAACAAJ&q=egyptian+colloquial+arabic|url-status=live}}</ref> It is part of the [[Afroasiatic languages|Afro-Asiatic language family]], and originated in the [[Nile Delta]] in [[Lower Egypt]]. The estimated 111&nbsp;million Egyptians speak a [[dialect continuum|continuum of dialects]].<ref name="e28|arz">{{e28|arz}}</ref> Egyptian Arabic is also known as Cairene Arabic.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cheddadi |first=Oualid |date=2025-01-12 |title=Egyptian Arabic: Everything You Need To Know |url=https://lingualid.com/egyptian-arabic/ |access-date=2026-05-31 |website=Lingualid |language=en-GB}}</ref> It is also understood across most of the [[Arab world|Arabic-speaking countries]] due to broad Egyptian influence in the region, including through [[Cinema of Egypt|Egyptian cinema]] and [[Music of Egypt|Egyptian music]]. These factors help make it the most widely spoken and by far the most widely studied [[Varieties of Arabic|variety of Arabic]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Dick|first=Marlin|title=TBS 15 The State of the Musalsal: Arab Television Drama and Comedy and the Politics of the Satellite Era by Marlin Dick|url=http://tbsjournal.arabmediasociety.com/Archives/Fall05/Dick.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304002737/http://tbsjournal.arabmediasociety.com/Archives/Fall05/Dick.html|archive-date=2016-03-04|access-date=2015-11-03|website=Arab Media & Society}}</ref><ref>Mahmoud Gaafar, Jane Wightwick (2014).''Colloquial Arabic of Egypt: The Complete Course for Beginners''.</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4R4DyZ8At7IC&q=egyptian+arabic+%22most%22&pg=PA173|title=The Europeans, Second Edition: A Geography of People, Culture, and Environment|last1=Ostergren|first1=Robert C.|last2=Bossé|first2=Mathias Le|date=2011-06-15|publisher=Guilford Press|isbn=978-1-60918-244-1|language=en|access-date=2020-10-25|archive-date=2023-03-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326133744/https://books.google.com/books?id=4R4DyZ8At7IC&q=egyptian+arabic+%22most%22&pg=PA173|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=socYatmLZc8C&q=egyptian+arabic+%22most%22&pg=PT1983|title=The Rough Guide to Egypt|last=Richardson|first=Dan|date=2007-08-02|publisher=Rough Guides UK|isbn=978-1-84836-798-2|language=en|access-date=2020-10-25|archive-date=2023-03-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326133828/https://books.google.com/books?id=socYatmLZc8C&q=egyptian+arabic+%22most%22&pg=PT1983|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://archive.org/details/culturecustomsof00mole|url-access=registration|page=[https://archive.org/details/culturecustomsof00mole/page/117 117]|quote=egyptian arabic influence.|title=Culture and Customs of Egypt|last=Asante|first=Molefi Kete|date=2002|publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group|isbn=978-0-313-31740-8|language=en}}</ref>

While it is primarily a spoken language, the written form is used in novels, plays and poems ([[vernacular literature]]), as well as in comics, advertising, some newspapers and transcriptions of popular songs. In most other written media and in radio and television news reporting, [[Modern Standard Arabic|literary Arabic]] is used. Literary Arabic is a standardized language based on the language of the [[Quran|Qur'an]], i.e. [[Classical Arabic]]. The Egyptian [[vernacular]] is almost universally written in the [[Arabic alphabet]] for local consumption, although it is commonly transcribed into [[Latin script|Latin letters]] or in the [[International Phonetic Alphabet]] in [[linguistics]] text and textbooks aimed at teaching non-native learners.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://tafl.live/|script-title=ar:تعلم العربية{{!}} جامعة الأزهر {{!}} بوابة التعليم الالكتروني والتعليم عن بعد {{!}} e-Learning Al-Azhar University {{!}} Learn Arabic|publisher=tafl.live|access-date=2019-11-05|archive-date=2020-11-30|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130234310/https://tafl.live/}}</ref> Egyptian Arabic's phonetics, grammatical structure, and vocabulary are influenced by the [[Coptic language]];{{sfn|Bishai|1962}}<ref>{{citation|title=لاف الكلمات القبطية في أحاديث المصريين اليومية|date=18 February 2017|url=https://raseef22.net/article/91686-%D8%A2%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%AB-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A#:~:text=%D8%AA%D8%A3%D8%AB%D9%8A%D8%B1%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9%20%D9%81%D9%8A%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9,%D9%88%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%A9%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AC%D9%85%D9%84%D8%A9%20%D9%88%D8%AA%D8%B1%D9%83%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%8C%20%D8%A8%D8%AD%D8%B3%D8%A8%20%D8%B3%D8%B9%D9%8A%D8%AF.|quote=ليس أثر القبطية في العامية في مصر مجرد تبادل مصطلحات، أو كلمات دخلت القاموس العربي كما دخلت كلمة "تلفزيون"، إنما تأثرت بها من حيث نطق الحروف وبنية الجملة وتركيبها – الدكتور لؤي محمود سعيد|access-date=30 October 2022|archive-date=30 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221030130738/https://raseef22.net/article/91686-%D8%A2%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AA-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%AB-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A#:~:text=%D8%AA%D8%A3%D8%AB%D9%8A%D8%B1%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9%20%D9%81%D9%8A%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9,%D9%88%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%A9%20%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AC%D9%85%D9%84%D8%A9%20%D9%88%D8%AA%D8%B1%D9%83%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%8C%20%D8%A8%D8%AD%D8%B3%D8%A8%20%D8%B3%D8%B9%D9%8A%D8%AF.|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{citation|url=https://www.masrawy.com/news/news-ayamzaman/details/2022/9/24/2296718/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%A7-%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%85%D8%A7-%D8%B2%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA-%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%A3%D8%AB%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D8%BA%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9|title=البابا تواضروس: العامية المصرية ما زالت متأثرة باللغة القبطية|website=masrawy.com|access-date=2022-10-21|archive-date=2022-10-21|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221021102841/https://www.masrawy.com/news/news-ayamzaman/details/2022/9/24/2296718/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%A7-%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%85%D8%A7-%D8%B2%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA-%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%A3%D8%AB%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D8%BA%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%8A%D8%A9|url-status=live}}</ref> its rich vocabulary is also influenced by [[Turkish language|Turkish]] and by [[Languages of Europe|European languages]] such as [[French language|French]], [[Italian language|Italian]], [[Greek language|Greek]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://raseef22.com/article/79624-13-%D9%84%D8%BA%D8%A9-%D8%A3%D8%AC%D9%86%D8%A8%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9-%D8%AA%D8%B4%D9%83%D9%91%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9|script-title=ar:13 لغة أجنبية تشكل العامية المصرية|trans-title=13 foreign languages within the Egyptian Arabic dialect|date=May 31, 2017|script-website=ar:رصيف 22|access-date=September 19, 2019|archive-date=August 14, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200814140127/https://raseef22.com/article/79624-13-%D9%84%D8%BA%D8%A9-%D8%A3%D8%AC%D9%86%D8%A8%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9-%D8%AA%D8%B4%D9%83%D9%91%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%91%D8%A9|url-status=live |work=رصيف 22 }}</ref> and [[English language|English]].

== Naming ==
Speakers of Egyptian Arabic generally call their vernacular ''Arabic'' ({{Lang|ar|عربى<!--Egyptian variant of final ى-->}}, {{IPA|arz|ˈʕɑrɑbi|}}) when juxtaposed with non-Arabic languages; ''Colloquial Egyptian'' ({{lang|arz|العاميه المصريه}}, {{IPA|arz|el.ʕæmˈmejjæ l.mɑsˤˈɾejjɑ|}}){{efn|<small>[[Classical Arabic]] pronunciation:</small> {{IPA|[alluʁˠatu lmisˠɾijjatu lʕaːmmijja]}}; <small>[[Modern Standard Arabic|Literary Arabic]]:</small> {{IPA|/alluɣatu lmisˤrijjatu lʕaːmmijja/}}.}} or simply ''Aamiyya'' ({{lang|arz|عاميه}}, ''colloquial'') when juxtaposed with [[Modern Standard Arabic]] and the ''Egyptian dialect'' ({{lang|arz|اللهجه المصريه}}, {{IPA|arz|elˈlæhɡæ l.mɑsˤˈɾejjɑ|}}){{efn|<small>[[Classical Arabic]] pronunciation:</small> {{IPA|[allahɟatu lmisˠɾijja]}}; <small>[[Modern Standard Arabic|Literary Arabic]]:</small> {{IPA|/allahɡatu lmisˤrijja/}}.}} or simply ''Masri'' ({{lang|arz|مصرى<!--Egyptian variant of final ى-->}}, {{IPA|arz|ˈmɑsˤɾi|}}, ''Egyptian'') when juxtaposed with other [[Varieties of Arabic|vernacular Arabic dialects]].<ref name="Islam online2">[http://www.islamonline.net/arabic/famous/2005/12/article04.shtml Islam online on Mahmoud Timor] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080724052458/http://www.islamonline.net/arabic/famous/2005/12/article04.shtml|date=July 24, 2008}}</ref>{{efn|<small>[[Classical Arabic]] pronunciation:</small> {{IPA|[alluʁˠatu lmisˠɾijjatu lħadiːθa]}}; <small>[[Modern Standard Arabic|Literary Arabic]]:</small> {{IPA|/alluɣatu lmisˤrijjatu lħadiːθa/}}.}}

The term ''Egyptian Arabic'' is usually used synonymously with ''Cairene Arabic'', which is technically a dialect of Egyptian Arabic. The country's native name, {{lang|arz|مصر}} {{transliteration|arz|ALA|Maṣr}}, is often used locally to refer to Cairo itself. As is the case with [[French of France#Paris|Parisian]] [[French language|French]], Cairene Arabic is by far the most prevalent dialect in the country.<ref>{{Citation|last=Kerstin|first=Odendahl|title=World Natural Heritage|date=August 2015|doi=10.1093/law:epil/9780199231690/e1950|encyclopedia=Max Planck Encyclopedia of Public International Law|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-923169-0|url=https://opil.ouplaw.com/view/10.1093/law:epil/9780199231690/law-9780199231690-e1950|access-date=2021-02-03|archive-date=2020-08-04|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804175539/https://opil.ouplaw.com/view/10.1093/law:epil/9780199231690/law-9780199231690-e1950|url-status=live|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

== Geographic distribution ==
Egyptian Arabic is used primarily in Egypt and is spoken predominantly in Cairo and Alexandria and in the capitals of all Egyptian governorates and in other major cities across Northern and Southern Egypt.

Egyptian Arabic has become widely understood in the Arabic-speaking world primarily for two reasons:{{sfn|Haeri|2003}}<ref>Jenkins, Siona. ''Egyptian Arabic Phrasebook''. [[Lonely Planet]] Publications, 2001. p. 205</ref> The proliferation and popularity of Egyptian films and other media in the region since the early 20th century as well as the great number of Egyptian teachers and professors who were instrumental in setting up the education systems of various countries in the [[Arabian Peninsula]] and also taught there and in other countries such as [[Algeria]] and [[Libya]]. Also, many [[Lebanese people|Lebanese]] artists choose to sing in Egyptian, prominent examples like [[Nancy Ajram]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Divas of the Middle East: The women who left a musical mark on the region |url=https://www.middleeasteye.net/discover/middle-east-divas-music-cinema-stars |access-date=2025-07-20 |website=Middle East Eye |language=en}}</ref> and [[Sabah (singer)|Sabah]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hadid |first=Diaa |date=2014-11-26 |title=Sabah, actress and entertainer who thrilled and scandalized the Arab world, dies at 87 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/sabah-actress-and-entertainer-who-thrilled-and-scandalized-the-arab-world-dies-at-87/2014/11/26/81ee8b88-7584-11e4-9d9b-86d397daad27_story.html |access-date=2025-07-20 |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

== History ==
{{more citations needed section|date=September 2020}}
[[Arabic]] was spoken in parts of [[Egypt]] such as the [[Eastern Desert]] and [[Sinai Peninsula|Sinai]] before Islam.<ref>''The History of Herodotus'' by George Rawlinson, p.e 9</ref> It also seems as though some Egypto-Arabic words derive from old Ancient Egyptian words.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024 |title=THE ORIGIN OF ARABIC WORDS IN THE ANCIENT EGYPTIAN LANGUAGE |url=https://digitalcommons.aaru.edu.jo/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1125&context=jguaa |access-date=1 Nov 2025 |website=digitalcommons.aaru.edu.jo|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240421221647/https://digitalcommons.aaru.edu.jo/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1125&context=jguaa|archive-date=2024-04-21}}</ref> However, Nile Valley [[Egyptians]] slowly adopted Arabic as a [[written language]] following the [[Muslim conquest of Egypt]] in the seventh century. Until then, they had spoken either [[Koine Greek]] or [[Egyptian language|Egyptian]] in its [[Coptic language|Coptic]] form. A period of Coptic-Arabic [[bilingualism]] in [[Lower Egypt]] lasted for more than three centuries. The period would last much longer in the south. Arabic had been already familiar to Valley Egyptians since Arabic had been spoken throughout the [[Eastern Desert]] and [[Sinai (peninsula)|Sinai]]. Arabic was also a minority language of some residents of the Nile Valley such as [[Qift]] in Upper Egypt through pre-Islamic trade with [[Nabateans]] in the [[Sinai Peninsula]] and the easternmost part of the [[Nile Delta]]. Egyptian Arabic seems to have begun taking shape in [[Fustat]], the first Islamic capital of Egypt, now part of [[Cairo]].

One of the earliest linguistic sketches of Cairene Arabic is a 16th-century document entitled ''{{transliteration|ar|DIN|Dafʿ al-ʾiṣr ʿan kalām ahl Miṣr}}''<ref>{{Cite book|last=Zack|first=Liesbeth|url=https://www.academia.edu/39310493|title=Edition of Daf' al-Isr دفع الإصر عن كلام أهل مصر|date=January 2009|language=en|access-date=2019-11-05|archive-date=2021-11-12|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112091746/https://www.academia.edu/39310493|url-status=live}}</ref>({{lang|ar|دفع الإصر عن كلام أهل مصر}}, "The Removal of the Burden from the Language of the People of Cairo") by the [[travel]]er and [[Lexicography|lexicographer]] [[Yusuf al-Maghribi]] ({{lang|ar|يوسف المغربي}}), with ''Misr'' here meaning "Cairo". It contains key information on early Cairene Arabic and the language situation in [[Egypt in the Middle Ages]]. The main purpose of the document was to show that while the Cairenes' vernacular contained many critical "errors" vis-à-vis Classical Arabic, according to al-Maghribi, it was also related to Arabic in other respects. With few waves of immigration from the Arabian peninsula such as the [[Banu Hilal]] exodus, who later left Egypt and were settled in Morocco and Tunisia, together with the ongoing [[Islamization]] and [[Arabization]] of the country, multiple Arabic varieties, one of which is Egyptian Arabic, slowly supplanted spoken Coptic. Local chroniclers mention the continued use of Coptic as a spoken language until the 17th century by peasant women in [[Upper Egypt]]. Coptic is still the [[liturgical language]] of the [[Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria]] and the [[Coptic Catholic Church]].

[[Ahmed Kamal (Egyptologist)|Ahmed Kamal Pasha]] (1851–1923), the author of Egypt's first Ancient Egyptian dictionary, referred to the fact that more than 12,000 words from the Modern Egyptian Arabic dialect are rooted in the Ancient Egyptian language.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-10-23 |title=From the BA to the British Museum: Ahmed Kamal Pasha's Dictionary |url=https://www.bibalex.org/en/News/Details?DocumentID=42534&Keywords= |access-date=2025-01-05 |website=Bibalex.org |language=en}}</ref> Kamal's efforts were groundbreaking, especially his assertion of linguistic connections between ancient Egyptian and Semitic languages, as both belong to the same [[Afroasiatic languages|Afro-Asiatic language]] tree.<ref>{{Citation |chapter=Ancient Egyptian |date=2013-07-11 |title=The Ancient Egyptian Language |pages=1–8 |chapter-url=https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139506090.003 |access-date=2025-05-31 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |doi=10.1017/cbo9781139506090.003 |isbn=978-1-107-03246-0|chapter-url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ezzat |first1=Azza |last2=Mansour |first2=Ahmed |date=2025-03-21 |title=Ahmed Kamal Pasha's Approach to Transliterate Egyptian Hieroglyphs in Arabic |url=https://brill.com/view/journals/abga/19/1/article-p54_7.xml |journal=Abgadiyat |volume=19 |issue=1 |pages=54–69 |doi=10.1163/22138609-01901007 |issn=2213-8609|url-access=subscription }}</ref> His methodology involved transliterating hieroglyphs into Arabic letters, making the study of ancient texts accessible to [[Egyptians]], who still carried the roots of their ancient language into the modern dialect.<ref>{{Cite news |title= Bibliotheca Alexandrina celebrates restored hieroglyphics-Arabic and French dictionary – Multimedia – Ahram Online|url=https://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContentMulti/506189/Multimedia.aspx}}</ref>

== Status ==
Egyptian Arabic has no official status and is not officially recognized as a language in [[Egypt]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=ISO 639-2 Language Code List - Codes for the representation of names of languages (Library of Congress) |url=https://www.loc.gov/standards/iso639-2/php/code_list.php |access-date=2024-08-09 |website=www.loc.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cover |first=Robin |title=Code for the Representation of the Names of Languages. From ISO 639, revised 1989. |url=https://xml.coverpages.org/iso639a.html |access-date=2024-08-09 |website=xml.coverpages.org}}</ref> [[Modern Standard Arabic|Standard Arabic]] is the official language of the state as per constitutional law with the name {{lang|ar|اللغة العربية}}, {{transliteration|ar|DIN|al-luġa al-ʿarabiyyah}}, {{lit|the Arabic language}}.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2017-04-03|title=الدستور المصري المعدل 2019|url=https://manshurat.org/node/14675|access-date=2020-06-16|website=منشورات قانونية|language=ar|archive-date=2020-04-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200428130413/https://manshurat.org/node/14675|url-status=live}}</ref> Interest in the local [[vernacular]] began in the 1800s (in opposition to the language of the ruling class, Turkish),{{Citation needed|date=August 2024}} as the Egyptian national movement for [[self-determination]] was taking shape. For many decades to follow, questions about the reform and the modernization of Arabic were hotly debated in Egyptian intellectual circles. Proposals ranged from developing [[neologism]]s to replace archaic terminology in Modern Standard Arabic to the simplification of syntactical and morphological rules and the introduction of [[colloquialism]]s to even complete "Egyptianization" ({{lang|ar|تمصير}}, {{lang|ar-Latn|tamṣīr}}) by abandoning the so-called Modern Standard Arabic in favor of Masri or Egyptian Arabic.<ref name="Gershoni">{{Cite book |author=Gershoni, I. |author2=J. Jankowski |title=Egypt, Islam, and the Arabs |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |location=Oxford |year=1987}}</ref>

Proponents of language reform in Egypt included [[Qasim Amin]], who also wrote the first Egyptian feminist treatise; former President of the [[Cairo University|Egyptian University]], [[Ahmed Lutfi el-Sayed]]; and noted intellectual [[Salama Moussa]]. They adopted a modernist, [[secular]] approach and disagreed with the assumption that Arabic was an immutable language because of its association with the [[Quran]]. The first modern Egyptian novel in which the dialogue was written in the vernacular was [[Muhammad Husayn Haykal]]'s ''[[Zaynab (novel)|Zaynab]]'' in 1913. It was only in 1966 that [[Ali Moustafa Mosharafa|Mustafa Musharafa]]'s ''Kantara Who Disbelieved'' was released, the first novel to be written entirely in Egyptian Arabic.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/18/62/50815/Books/Review/Book-Review-First-novel-written-in-colloquial-Arab.aspx|title=Book Review: First novel written in colloquial Arabic republished – Review – Books – Ahram Online|access-date=2013-04-19|archive-date=2013-01-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130119102454/http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/18/62/50815/Books/Review/Book-Review-First-novel-written-in-colloquial-Arab.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref> Other notable novelists, such as [[Ihsan Abdel Quddous]] and [[Yusuf Idris]], and poets, such as [[Salah Jahin]], [[Abdel Rahman el-Abnudi]] and [[Ahmed Fouad Negm]], helped solidify vernacular literature as a distinct literary genre.<ref name="Gershoni" />

Amongst certain groups within Egypt's elite, Egyptian Arabic enjoyed a brief period of rich literary output. That dwindled with the rise of [[Pan-Arabism]], which had gained popularity in Egypt by the second half of the twentieth century, as demonstrated by Egypt's involvement in the [[1948 Arab–Israeli War]] under King [[Farouk of Egypt]]. The [[Egyptian revolution of 1952]], led by [[Mohamed Naguib]] and [[Gamal Abdel Nasser]], further enhanced the significance of Pan-Arabism, making it a central element of Egyptian state policy. The importance of Modern Standard Arabic was reemphasised in the public sphere by the revolutionary government, and efforts to accord any formal language status to the Egyptian vernacular were ignored. Egyptian Arabic was identified as a mere dialect, one that was not spoken even in all of Egypt, as almost all of [[Upper Egypt]] speaks [[Sa'idi Arabic]]. Though the revolutionary government heavily sponsored the use of the Egyptian vernacular in films, plays, television programmes, and music, the prerevolutionary use of Modern Standard Arabic in official publications was retained.{{Citation needed|reason=Reliable source needed for the whole paragraph|date=July 2020}}

Linguistic commentators{{who|date=July 2020}} have noted the multi-faceted approach of the Egyptian revolutionaries towards the Arabic language. Whereas Naguib, Egypt's first [[president of Egypt|president]], exhibited a preference for using Modern Standard Arabic in his public speeches, his successor Gamal Abdel Nasser was renowned for using the vernacular and for punctuating his speeches with traditional Egyptian words and expressions. Conversely, Modern Standard Arabic was the norm for state news outlets, including newspapers, magazines, television, and radio. That was especially true of Egypt's national broadcasting company, the [[ERTU|Arab Radio and Television Union]], which was established with the intent of providing content for the entire [[Arab world]], not merely Egypt, hence the need to broadcast in the standard, rather than the vernacular, language. The [[Voice of the Arabs]] radio station, in particular, had an audience from across the region, and the use of anything other than Modern Standard Arabic was viewed as eminently incongruous.

In a study of three Egyptian newspapers ([[Al-Ahram]], [[Al-Masry Al-Youm]], and [[Al-Dustour (Egypt)|Al-Dustour]]) Zeinab Ibrahim concluded that the total number of headlines in Egyptian Arabic in each newspaper varied. Al-Ahram did not include any. Al-Masry Al-Youm had an average of 5% of headlines in Egyptian, while Al-Dustour averaged 11%.<ref>{{cite book | first=Zeinab |last=Ibrahim| title=Arabic and the Media | chapter=Cases Of Written Code-Switching In Egyptian Opposition Newspapers | publisher=BRILL | date=1 January 2011 | doi=10.1163/ej.9789004182585.i-303.17 | pages=23–45|isbn=978-90-04-18761-0}}</ref>

As the status of Egyptian Arabic as opposed to [[Classical Arabic]] can have such political and religious implications in Egypt,{{how|date=July 2020}} the question of whether Egyptian Arabic should be considered a [[Dialect#Dialect or language|"dialect" or "language"]] can be a source of debate. In [[sociolinguistics]], Egyptian Arabic can be seen as one of many distinct [[variety (linguistics)|varieties]] that, despite arguably being languages on {{lang|de|[[Abstand and ausbau languages|abstand]]}} grounds, are united{{How|date=July 2021}}{{According to whom|date=July 2021}} by a common {{lang|de|[[abstand and ausbau languages#Roofing|Dachsprache]]}} in Modern Standard Arabic (MSA).

== Publications ==
During the early 1900s many portions of the Bible were published in Egyptian Arabic.  These were published by the ''Nile Mission Press''.  By 1932 the whole New Testament and some books of the Old Testament had been published in Egyptian Arabic in Arabic script.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The British Civil Engineer who made Jesus speak like an Egyptian: William Willcocks and al-Khabar al-Ṭayyib bitāʿ Yasūʿ al-Masīḥ – Biblia Arabica|url=https://biblia-arabica.com/the-british-civil-engineer-who-made-jesus-speak-like-an-egyptian-william-willcocks-and-al-khabar-al-%e1%b9%adayyib-bita%ca%bf-yasu%ca%bf-al-masi%e1%b8%a5/|last=Binder {{!}}|first=Adrian|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-27|archive-date=2020-08-15|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200815040022/https://biblia-arabica.com/the-british-civil-engineer-who-made-jesus-speak-like-an-egyptian-william-willcocks-and-al-khabar-al-%E1%B9%ADayyib-bita%CA%BF-yasu%CA%BF-al-masi%E1%B8%A5/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The dialogs in the following novels are partly in Egyptian Arabic, partly in Standard Arabic: [[Mahmud Tahir Haqqi]]'s ''Adhra' Dinshuway'' ({{langx |ar|عذراء دنشواي}}; 1906), [[Yaqub Sarruf]]'s ''Fatat Misr'' ({{langx |ar|فتاة مصر |Fatāt Miṣr  |translit-std=ALA}}; first published in [[Al-Muqtataf (magazine)|Al-Muqtataf]] 1905–1906), and [[Mohammed Hussein Heikal]]'s ''[[Zaynab (novel)|Zaynab]]'' (1914).{{sfn|Diem|1974|p=109}}

Early stage plays written in Egyptian Arabic were translated from or influenced by European playwrights. [[Muhammad 'Uthman Jalal]] translated plays by [[Molière]], [[Jean Racine]] and [[Carlo Goldoni]] to Egyptian Arabic and adapted them as well as ten fables by [[Jean de La Fontaine]]. [[Yaqub Sanu]] translated to and wrote plays on himself in Egyptian Arabic.{{sfn|Woidich|2010|p=70}}
Many plays were written in Standard Arabic, but performed in colloquial Arabic. [[Tawfiq al-Hakim]] took this a step further and provided for his Standard Arabic plays versions in colloquial Arabic for the performances.{{sfn|Diem|1974|p=102}} [[Mahmud Taymur]] has published some of his plays in two versions, one in Standard, one in colloquial Arabic, among them: ''Kidb fi Kidb'' ({{langx |ar|كذب في كذب |lit=All lies}}, 1951{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=601}} or ca. 1952) and ''Al-Muzayyifun'' ({{langx |ar|المزيفون |Al-Muzayyifūn |translit-std=ALA |lit=The Forgers}}, ca. 1953).{{sfn|Diem|1974|p=116}}

The writers of stage plays in Egyptian Arabic after the [[Egyptian Revolution of 1952]] include [[No'man Ashour]], [[Alfred Farag]], {{ill|Saad Eddin Wahba|ar|سعد الدين وهبة<!-- |WD=Q12215902 -->}}, [[Rashad Roushdy]], and [[Yusuf Idris]].{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=601}} Thereafter the use of colloquial Egyptian Arabic in theater is stable and common.{{sfn|Woidich|2010|p=74}} Later writers of plays in colloquial Egyptian include [[Ali Salem]], and [[Naguib Surur]].{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=601}}

Novels in Egyptian Arabic after the 1940s and before the 1990s are rare. There are by {{ill|Mustafa Musharrafah|ar|مصطفى مصطفى مشرفة<!-- |WD=Q12243450 -->}} ''Qantarah Alladhi Kafar'' ({{langx |ar|قنطرة الذي كفر|Qanṭarah Alladhī Kafar |translit-std=ALA |lit=Qantara Who Disbelieved |label=none}}, Cairo, 1965) and Uthman Sabri's ({{langx |ar| عثمان صبري |ʻUthmān Ṣabrī |translit-std=ALA}}; 1896–1986) ''Journey on the Nile'' ({{langx |arz|رحلة في النيل |Riḥlah fī il-Nīl |translit-std=ALA}}, 1965){{sfn|Zack|2001|p=194}} (and his ''Bet Sirri'' ({{langx |arz|بيت سري |Bēt Sirri |translit-std=ALA |lit=A Brothel |label=none}}, 1981) that apparently uses a mix of Standard Arabic and Egyptian Arabic{{sfn|Woidich|2010|p=77|loc=footnote 66}}).

Prose published in Egyptian Arabic since the 1990s include the following novels:
[[Yusuf al-Qa'id]]'s ''Laban il-Asfur'' ({{langx |arz|لبن العصفور |Laban il-ʿAṣfūr |translit-std=ALA |lit=The Milk of the Bird |label=none}}; 1994),{{sfn|Woidich|2010|p=79}}
Baha' Awwad's ({{langx |ar|بهاء عواد |Bahāʾ ʿAwwād |translit-std=ALA}}) ''Shams il-Asil'' ({{langx |arz|شمس الاصيل |Shams il-ʿAṣīl |translit-std=ALA |lit=Late Afternoon Sun |label=none}}; 1998),
[[Safa Abdel Al Moneim]]'s ''Min Halawit il-Ruh'' ({{langx |arz|من حلاوة الروح |Min Ḥalāwit il-Rōḥ |translit-std=ALA |lit=Zest for Life |label=none}}, 1998),
Samih Faraj's<!-- =https://elcinema.com/en/person/2080919/ ? --> ({{langx |ar|سامح فرج |Sāmiḥ Faraj |translit-std=ALA}}) ''Banhuf Ishtirasa'' ({{langx |arz|بانهوف اشتراسا |Bānhūf Ishtirāsā |translit-std=ALA |lit=Bahnhof Strasse |label=none}}, 1999);
autobiographies include the one by [[Ahmed Fouad Negm]],
by {{ill|Mohammed Naser Ali|ar|محمد ناصر (إعلامي)<!-- |WD=Q47463303 -->}} ''Ula Awwil'' ({{langx |arz|اولى أول |Ūlá Awwil |translit-std=ALA |lit=First Class Primary School |label=none}}),
and [[Fathia al-Assal]]'s ''Hudn il-Umr'' ({{langx |arz|حضن العمر |Ḥuḍn il-ʿUmr |translit-std=ALA |lit=The Embrace of a Lifetime |label=none}}).{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=599}}{{sfn|Woidich|2010|pp=82–83}}

The [[epistolary novel]] ''Jawabat Haraji il-Gutt'' ({{langx |aec|جوابات حراجى القط |Jawābāt Ḥarājī il-Guṭṭ |translit-std=ALA |lit=Letters of Haraji the Cat}}, 2001) by [[Abdel Rahman el-Abnudi]] is exceptional in its use of [[Saʽidi Arabic]].{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=599}}

21st-century journals publishing in Egyptian Arabic include ''Bārti'' (from at least 2002), the weekly magazine ''Idhak lil-Dunya'' ({{langx |ar|اضحك للدنيا |Iḍḥak lil-Dunyā |translit-std=ALA |lit=Smile for the World |label=none}}, from 2005),{{sfn|Davies|2005|p=600}}{{sfn|Woidich|2010|pp=84–85}} and the monthly magazine {{ill|Ihna (magazine)|lt=Ihna|ar|إحنا (مجلة)<!-- |WD=Q12181941 -->|italic=y}} ({{langx |arz|احنا |Iḥna |translit-std=ALA |lit=We |label=none}}, from 2005).{{sfn|Borg|2007}} In the 21st century the number of books published in Egyptian Arabic has increased a lot. Many of them are by female authors, for example ''[[Wanna Be a Bride|I Want to Get Married!]]'' ({{langx |arz|عايزه أتجوز |ʻĀyzah atgawwiz |translit-std=ALA |label=none}}, 2008) by [[Ghada Abdel Aal]] and ''She Must Have Travelled'' ({{langx |arz|شكلها سافرت |Shaklahā sāfarit |translit-std=ALA |label=none}}, 2016) by Soha Elfeqy.

== Spoken varieties ==
[[Saʽidi Arabic]] is a sub-dialect of Egyptian Arabic spoken primarily in Upper Egypt.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Learn Palestinian Arabic Online |url=https://playaling.com/dialects/egyptian-arabic/saidi/ |access-date=2026-05-30 |website=Playaling |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>David Dalby, 1999/2000, ''The Linguasphere Register'', The Linguasphere Observatory<br />William Bright, 1992, ''The International Encyclopedia of Linguistics'', Oxford.</ref> It carries little prestige nationally but continues to be widely spoken.<ref>{{cite web|title=Arabic, Sa'idi Spoken|url=https://www.ethnologue.com/language/aec|website=Ethnologue|access-date=2020-06-06|archive-date=2020-06-10|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200610043947/https://www.ethnologue.com/language/aec|url-status=live}}</ref>

The traditional division between [[Upper and Lower Egypt]] and their respective differences go back to ancient times. Egyptians today commonly call the people of the delta بحاروة, Baharwa ({{IPA|[bɑˈħɑɾwɑ]}}) and those of the south صعايدة, [[Sa'idi people|Sa’ayda]] ({{IPA|[sˤɑˈʕɑjdɑ]}}). The differences throughout Egypt, however, are more wide-ranging and do not neatly correspond to the simple division. Despite the differences, there are features distinguishing all the Egyptian Arabic varieties of the Nile Valley from any other varieties of Arabic. Such features include [[vowel reduction|reduction of long vowels]] in open and unstressed syllables, the postposition of demonstratives and interrogatives, the modal meaning of the imperfect and the integration of the participle.<ref>Versteegh, p. 162</ref>

The [[Western Egyptian Bedawi Arabic]] variety<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=ayl|title=Arabic, Libyan Spoken|access-date=2010-09-08|archive-date=2011-05-14|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514044722/http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=ayl|url-status=live}}</ref> of the western desert differs from all other Arabic varieties in Egypt in that it linguistically is part of [[Maghrebi Arabic]].<ref>David Dalby, 1999/2000, ''The Linguasphere Register'', The Linguasphere Observatory</ref> [[Northwest Arabian Arabic]] is also distinct from Egyptian Arabic.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=avl|title=Arabic, Eastern Egyptian Bedawi Spoken|access-date=2010-09-08|archive-date=2011-05-14|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514044846/http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=avl|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Regional variations ===

Egyptian Arabic varies regionally across its [[sprachraum]], with certain characteristics being noted as typical of the speech of certain regions.

==== Alexandria ====
The dialect of [[Alexandria]] (West Delta) is noted for certain [[shibboleth]]s separating its speech from that of Cairo (South Delta). The ones that are most frequently noted in popular discourse are the use of the word ''[[falafel]]'' as opposed to {{lang|arz|طعميّة}} {{transliteration|arz|ALA|taʿmiyya}} for the fava-bean fritters common across the country and the pronunciation of the word for the [[Egyptian pound]] ({{lang|arz|جنيه}} {{transliteration|arz|ALA|ginēh}} {{IPA|arz|ɡeˈneː|}}), as {{IPA|arz|ˈɡeni|}}, closer to the pronunciation of the origin of the term, the British [[guinea (British coin)|guinea]]).
The speech of the older Alexandrians is also noted for use of the same pre-syllable (ne-) in the singular and plural of the first person present and future tenses, which is also a common feature of [[Tunisian Arabic]]<ref>{{citation|title=Tunisian Arabic Grammar|url=https://lingualism.com/maghrebi-arabic/tunisian-arabic-grammar/|website=lingualism.com|date=27 June 2018|access-date=29 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230204210626/https://lingualism.com/maghrebi-arabic/tunisian-arabic-grammar/|archive-date=2023-02-04}}</ref><ref>Cf. also {{Cite Q |Q58152596 |last=Owens |first=Jonathan}}.</ref> and of [[Maghrebi Arabic]] in general. The dialects of the western Delta tend to use the perfect with {{IPAslink|a}} instead of the perfect with {{IPAslink|i}}, for example for {{lang|arz|فهم}} this is {{transliteration|arz|ALA|faham}} instead of {{transliteration|arz|ALA|fihim}}.{{sfn|Woidich|2006|p=62|loc=2.3.2.1.1 a-Perfekt und i-Perfekt}} Other examples for this are {{langx |arz|لَبَس |labas |translit-std=ALA |lit=to wear |label=none}}, {{langx |arz|نَزَل |nazal |translit-std=ALA |lit=to descend |label=none}}, {{langx |arz|شَرَب |sharab |translit-std=ALA |lit=to drink |label=none}}, {{langx |arz|نَسَى |nasá |translit-std=ALA |lit=to forget |label=none}},{{sfn|Behnstedt|Woidich|2018|p=78|loc=3.3.2 The Case of Alexandria}} رَجَع, طَلَع, رَكَب.

==== Port Said ====
[[Port Said]]'s dialect (East Delta) is noted for a "heavier", more [[guttural]] sound, compared to other regions of the country.

==== Rural Nile Delta ====
The dialect of Rural Nile Delta in Northern Egypt is noted for a distinct accent, replacing the urban pronunciations of {{IPAslink|ɡ}} (spelled {{lang|ar|[[ج]]}} {{transliteration|arz|gīm}}) and {{IPAslink|q}} ({{lang|ar|[[ق]]}} {{transliteration|arz|qāf}}) with {{IPAblink|ʒ}} and {{IPAblink|ɡ}} respectively, but that is not true of all rural dialects, some of them do not have such replacement. The dialect also has many grammatical differences when contrasted to urban dialects.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Woidich|first=Manfred|date=1996-12-31|title=Rural Dialect of Egyptian Arabic: An Overview|url=http://journals.openedition.org/ema/1952|journal=Égypte/Monde arabe|language=en|issue=27–28|pages=325–354|doi=10.4000/ema.1952|issn=1110-5097|doi-access=free|access-date=2021-04-20|archive-date=2021-04-23|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210423164550/https://journals.openedition.org/ema/1952|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Phonology ==
{{Main|Egyptian Arabic phonology}}

Egyptian Arabic has a phonology that differs significantly from that of other varieties of Arabic, and has its own inventory of consonants and vowels.

== Morphology ==

=== Nouns ===
In contrast to CA and MSA, but like all modern colloquial [[varieties of Arabic]], Egyptian Arabic nouns are not inflected for case and lack [[nunation]] (with the exception of certain fixed phrases in the accusative case, such as {{lang|ar|شكراً}} {{IPA|[ˈʃokɾɑn]}}, "thank you"). As all nouns take their [[pausal pronunciation|pausal]] forms, singular words and [[broken plural]]s simply lose their case endings. In sound plurals and dual forms, where, in MSA, difference in case is present even in pausal forms, the genitive/accusative form is the one preserved. Fixed expressions in the [[construct state]] beginning in ''abu'', often geographic names, retain their ''-u'' in all cases.<ref>See e.g. Behnstedt & Woidich (2005)</ref>

==== Plurals ====
Nouns take either a ''sound plural'' or ''[[broken plural]]''. The sound plural is formed by adding endings, and can be considered part of the declension. For the broken plural, however, a different pattern for the stem is used.
The sound plural with the suffix {{langx |arz|ـِين |-īn |translit-std=ALA |label=none}} is used for nouns referring to male persons that are participles or follow the pattern CaCCaaC. It takes the form {{langx |arz|ـيِين |-yīn |translit-std=ALA |label=none}} for nouns of the form CaCCa and the form {{langx |arz|ـيِّين |-yyīn |translit-std=ALA |label=none}} for ''nisba'' adjectives.{{sfn|Woidich|2006|loc=2.4.9.3.1 Externe Pluralbildung mit ''-īn'', ''-āt'' and ''-a''|p=115}}

