=== Negation ===
{{Main|Negation in Arabic}}

One characteristic feature of Egyptian syntax is the two-part negative verbal circumfix {{IPA|/ma-...-ʃ(i)/}}, which it shares with other North African dialect areas as well as some [[South Levantine Arabic|southern Levantine]] dialect areas, probably as a result of the influence of Egyptian Arabic on these areas:

* Past: {{IPA|/ˈkatab/}} "he wrote" {{IPA|/ma-katab-ʃ(i)/}} "he didn't write" {{lang|arz|ما كتبشِ}}
* Present: {{IPA|/ˈbijik-tib/}} "he writes" {{IPA|/ma-bjik-tib-ʃ(i)/}} "he doesn't write" {{lang|arz|ما بيكتبشِ}}

{{IPA|/ma-/}} probably comes from the Arabic negator {{IPA|/maː/}}. This negating [[circumfix]] is similar in function to the [[French language|French]] circumfix {{lang|fr|ne ... pas}}. It should also be noted that Coptic and Ancient Egyptian both had negative circumfix.

The structure can end in a consonant {{IPA|/ʃ/}} or in a vowel {{IPA|/i/}}, varying according to the individual or region. Nowadays speakers use {{IPA|/ʃ/}}. However, {{IPA|/ʃi/}} was sometimes used stylistically, specially in the past, as attested in [[Egyptian films of the 1940s|old films]].

The negative circumfix often surrounds the entire verbal composite including direct and indirect object pronouns:
* {{IPA|/ma-katab-hum-ˈliː-ʃ/}}  "he didn't write them to me"
However, verbs in the future tense can instead use the prefix /miʃ/:
* {{IPA|/miʃ-ħa-ˈjiktib/}} (or {{IPA|/ma-ħa-jikˈtibʃ/}}  "he won't write"
Interrogative sentences can be formed by adding the negation [[clitic]] "(miʃ)" before the verb:
* Past: {{IPA|/ˈkatab/}} "he wrote"; {{IPA|/miʃ-ˈkatab/}} "didn't he write?"
* Present: {{IPA|/ˈjiktib/}} "he writes"; {{IPA|/miʃ-bi-ˈjiktib/}} "doesn't he write?"
* Future: {{IPA|/ħa-ˈjiktib/}} "he will write"; {{IPA|/miʃ-ħa-ˈjiktib/}} "won't he write?"
Addition of the circumfix can cause complex changes to the verbal cluster, due to the application of the rules of vowel syncope, shortening, lengthening, insertion and elision described above:
* The addition of /ma-/ may trigger elision or syncope:
**A vowel following /ma-/ is elided: (ixtáːr) "he chose" → ({{IPA|maxtárʃ}}).
**A short vowel /i/ or /u/ in the first syllable may be deleted by syncope: (kíbir) "he grew" → (makbírʃ).
* The addition of {{IPA|/-ʃ/}} may result in vowel shortening or epenthesis:
** A final long vowel preceding a single consonant shortens: (ixtáːr) "he chose" → (maxtárʃ).
** An unstressed epenthetic /i/ is inserted when the verbal complex ends in two consonants: /kunt/ "I was" → (makúntiʃ).
* In addition, the addition of {{IPA|/-ʃ/}} triggers a stress shift, which may in turn result in vowel shortening or lengthening:
** The stress shifts to the syllable preceding {{IPA|/ʃ/}}: (kátab) "he wrote" → (makatábʃ).
** A long vowel in the previously stressed syllable shortens: (ʃáːfit) "she saw" → (maʃafítʃ); (ʃá:fu) "they saw" ''or'' "he saw it" → (maʃafú:ʃ).
** A final short vowel directly preceding {{IPA|/ʃ/}} lengthens: (ʃáːfu) "they saw" ''or'' "he saw it" → (maʃafú:ʃ).

In addition, certain other morphological changes occur:
* (ʃafúː) "they saw him" → (maʃafuhúːʃ) (to avoid a clash with (maʃafúːʃ) "they didn't see/he didn't see him").
* (ʃáːfik) "He saw you (fem. sg.)" → (maʃafkíːʃ).
* (ʃúftik) "I saw you (fem. sg.)" → (maʃuftikíːʃ).

== Syntax ==
In contrast with Classical Arabic, but much like the other [[varieties of Arabic]], Egyptian Arabic prefers [[subject–verb–object]] (SVO) [[word order]]; CA and to a lesser extent MSA prefer [[verb–subject–object]] (VSO). For example, in MSA "Adel read the book" would be {{lang|ar|قرأَ عادل الكتاب}} ''{{transliteration|ar|DIN|Qaraʾa ʿĀdilu l-kitāb}}'' <!--MSA as pronounced by Egyptians:-->{{IPA|ar|ˈqɑɾɑʔɑ ˈʕæːdel ol keˈtæːb|IPA}} whereas EA would say {{lang|arz|عادل قرا الكتاب}} ''{{transliteration|arz|ʕādil ʔara l-kitāb}}'' {{IPA|arz|ˈʕæːdel ˈʔɑɾɑ lkeˈtæːb|IPA}}.

Also in common with other Arabic varieties is the loss of unique [[agreement (linguistics)|agreement]] in the dual form: while the dual remains productive to some degree in nouns, dual nouns are analyzed as plural for the purpose of agreement with verbs, demonstratives, and adjectives. Thus "These two Syrian professors are walking to the university" in MSA (in an SVO sentence for ease of comparison) would be "{{lang|ar|هذان الأستاذان السوريان يمشيان إلى الجامعة}}" ''{{transliteration|ar|DIN|Haḏān al-ʾustāḏān as-Sūriyyān yamšiyān ʾilā l-ǧāmiʿah}}'' <!--MSA as pronounced by Egyptians:-->{{IPA|ar|hæːˈzæːn æl ʔostæːˈzæːn as suːrejˈjæːn jæmʃeˈjæːn ˈʔelæ lɡæːˈmeʕæ|IPA}}, which becomes in EA "{{lang|arz|الأستاذين السوريين دول بيمشو للجامعة}}" ''{{transliteration|arz|il-ʔustazēn il-Suriyyīn dōl biyimʃu lil-gamʕa}},'' {{IPA|arz|el ʔostæˈzeːn el soɾejˈjiːn ˈdoːl beˈjemʃo lelˈɡæmʕæ|IPA}}.

Unlike most other forms of Arabic, however, Egyptian prefers final placement of [[question word]]s in interrogative sentences. This is a feature characteristic of the [[#Coptic substratum|Coptic substratum of Egyptian Arabic]].

== Coptic substratum ==
{{see also|Copto-Arabic literature}}
Some authors have argued for the influence of a [[stratum (linguistics)|substratum]] of the [[Coptic language]] which was the native language of the vast majority of [[Nile Valley]] Egyptians prior to the Muslim invasion on Egyptian Arabic,{{sfn|Nishio|1995}}{{sfn|Bishai|1962}}{{sfn|Youssef|2003}} specifically on its [[phonology]], [[syntax]], and [[lexicon]]. Coptic is the latest stage of the indigenous [[Egyptian language]] spoken until the mid-17th century when it was finally completely supplanted among Egyptian Muslims and a majority of Copts by the Egyptian Arabic.

=== Phonology ===
Since Coptic lacked [[interdental consonant]]s it could possibly have influenced the manifestation of their occurrences in [[Classical Arabic]] {{IPAslink|θ}} {{IPAslink|ð}} {{IPAslink|ðˤ}} as their dental counterparts {{IPAslink|t}} {{IPAslink|d}} and the emphatic dental {{IPAslink|dˤ}} respectively. (see [[Egyptian Arabic phonology#Consonants|consonants]])

Behnstedt argues that the phenomenon of merging of interdentals with plosives has also occurred in areas without a substratum lacking interdentals, e.g. in [[Mecca]], [[Aden]] and [[Bahrain]], and can be caused by drift rather than the influence of a substratum concluding that "[o]n the phonological level, there is no evidence for Coptic substratal influence."{{sfn|Behnstedt|2005|p=501}}

=== Syntax ===
A syntactic feature of to Egyptian Arabic arguably inherited from Coptic{{sfn|Nishio|1995}} is the [[wh-in-situ|remaining]] of [[interrogative word]]s (i.e. "who", "when", "why") in their "logical" positions in a sentence rather than being preposed, or moved to the front of the sentence, as (mostly) in Classical Arabic or English.

Examples:
* {{lang|arz|راح مصر امتى؟}} {{IPA|/rˤaːħ masˤr ʔimta/}} "When ({{IPA|/ʔimta/}}) did he go to Egypt?" (lit. "He went to Egypt when?")
* {{lang|arz|راح مصر ليه؟}} {{IPA|/rˤaːħ masˤr leːh/}} "Why ({{IPA|/leːh/}}) did he go to Egypt? (lit. "He went to Egypt why?")
* {{lang|arz|مين [اللى] راح مصر؟}} {{IPA|/miːn rˤaːħ masˤr/}} or {{IPA|/miːn illi rˤaːħ masˤr/}} "Who ({{IPA|/miːn/}}) went to Egypt/Cairo? (literally – same order)
The same sentences in [[Modern Standard Arabic|Literary Arabic]] (with all the question words (''wh''-words) in the beginning of the sentence) would be:
* {{lang|ar|متى ذهب إلى مصر؟}} {{IPA|/mataː ðahaba ʔilaː misˤr/}}
* {{lang|ar|لِمَ ذهب إلى مصر؟}} {{IPA|/lima ðahaba ʔilaː misˤr/}}
* {{lang|ar|من ذهب إلى مصر؟}} {{IPA|/man ðahaba ʔilaː misˤr/}}

Diem argues that in Cairene Arabic also the preposition of interrogative words occurs and in Classical Arabic and other Arabic dialects also their postposition and thus the effect of a Coptic substratum might be – if any – the preference for one of the two possibilities.{{sfn|Diem|1979|pp=51–52}}

=== Lexicon ===
Behnstedt estimates the existence of ca. 250 to 300 Coptic loanwords in Egyptian Arabic.{{sfn|Behnstedt|2005|p=503}}

== Orthography and romanization ==
=== Orthography ===
There is no fixed orthography for Egyptian Arabic.
Where it is written in Arabic script the orthography varies between spellings closer to those of Standard Arabic and spellings closer to the phonology of Egyptian Arabic.
This variability arises from the deficiency of the Arabic script for writing the colloquial Egyptian Arabic, for which it is not designed. Part of this is the unavailability of signs for some sounds of Egyptian Arabic that are not part of Standard Arabic.{{sfn|Zack|2001|p=199|loc=1. The orthography}}
Both options are used in parallel, often even in by one author or in one work.{{sfn|Woidich|2006|loc=Einleitung|p=2}}{{sfn|Zack|2001|pp=199–200|loc=a. Description of the orthography of ''Laban il-'aṣfūr''}}
The two options appears for example for these cases:
* treatment of originally long vowels that become short or deleted as a result of [[Egyptian Arabic phonology#Vowel shortening|vowel shortening]] or [[Egyptian Arabic phonology#Vowel deletion (syncope)|vowel deletion]], e.g. the feminine active participle of {{langx |arz|عرف |ʿirif |translit-std=ALA |lit=to know |label=none}}, that is pronounced {{IPA|arz|ˈʕæɾfæ|}}, can be written in two ways:
** etymological spelling with the shortly pronounced originally long vowel "ا": {{lang|arz|عارفة}},
** phonetic spelling without the "ا": {{lang|arz|عرفة}};
* words written with the letters "ث"&lrm;, "ذ", and "ظ" in Standard Arabic that are pronounced {{IPAslink|t}}, {{IPAslink|z}}, and {{IPAslink|dˁ}} in Egyptian Arabic can keep their etymological Standard Arabic spelling or be phonetically respelled with "ت"&lrm;, "د" and "ض".

