== Sociolinguistic features ==
Egyptian Arabic is used in most social situations, with Modern Standard and Classical Arabic generally being used only in writing and in highly religious and/or formal situations. However, within Egyptian Arabic, there is a wide range of variation. [[El-Said Badawi]] identifies three distinct levels of Egyptian Arabic-based chiefly on the quantity of non-Arabic lexical items in the vocabulary: ''ʿĀmmiyyat al-Musaqqafīn'' (Cultured Colloquial or [[Formal Spoken Arabic]]), ''ʿĀmmiyyat al-Mutanawwirīn'' (Enlightened or Literate Colloquial), and ''ʿĀmmiyyat al-'Ummiyīn'' (Illiterate Colloquial).{{sfn|Hinds|Badawi|1986|pp=VII–X}} Cultured Colloquial/Formal Spoken Arabic is characteristic of the educated classes and is the language of discussion of high-level subjects, but it is still Egyptian Arabic; it is characterized by use of technical terms imported from foreign languages and MSA and closer attention to the pronunciation of certain letters (particularly ''[[ق|qāf]]''). It is relatively standardized and, being closer to the standard, it is understood fairly well across the [[Arab world]].{{sfn|Hinds|Badawi|1986|pp=VII–X}} On the opposite end of the spectrum, Illiterate Colloquial, common to rural areas and to working-class neighborhoods in the cities, has an almost-exclusively Arabic vocabulary; the few loanwords generally are very old borrowings (e.g. {{lang|arz|جمبرى}} ''{{transliteration|arz|gambari}}'', {{IPA|[ɡæmˈbæɾi]}} "[[shrimp]]", from [[Italian language|Italian]] ''gamberi'', "shrimp" (pl.)) or refer to technological items that find no or poor equivalents in Arabic (e.g. {{lang|arz|تلفزيون}} ''{{transliteration|arz|tel(e)vezyōn/tel(e)fezyōn}}'' {{IPA|[tel(e)vezˈjoːn, tel(e)fezˈjoːn]}}, [[television]]).{{sfn|Hinds|Badawi|1986|pp=VII–X}} Enlightened Colloquial (''ʿĀmmiyyat al-Mutanawwirīn'') is the language of those who have had some schooling and are relatively affluent; loanwords tend to refer to items of popular culture, consumer products, and fashions. It is also understood widely in the Arab world, as it is the ''[[lingua franca]]'' of [[Egyptian cinema]] and television.{{sfn|Hinds|Badawi|1986|pp=VII–X}}

In contrast to MSA and most other varieties of Arabic, Egyptian Arabic has a form of the [[T-V distinction]]. In the singular, {{lang|arz|انت}} ''enta''/''enti'' is acceptable in most situations, but to address clear social superiors (e.g. older persons, superiors at work, certain government officials), the form {{lang|arz|حضرتك}} ''{{transliteration|arz|ḥaḍretak/ḥaḍretek}}'', meaning "Your [[Grace (style)|Grace]]" is preferred (compare [[Spanish language|Spanish]] ''[[Spanish pronouns#The use of vusted and vuestra merced|usted]]'').

This use of ''{{transliteration|arz|ḥaḍretak/ḥaḍretek}}'' is linked to the system of [[honorifics]] in daily Egyptian speech. The honorific taken by a given person is determined by their relationship to the speaker and their occupation.

